#lang treason

;; Slide: "Limitations: SSE in Incomplete Context"
;;
;; The slide reuses the my-define example. This file uses the example the
;; talk notes suggest instead, since it shows the limitation directly. The bad
;; my-let use is missing x's expression, so treason expands the body on its
;; own, in the context of the use. The binding of x would come from inside the
;; use, so in that context x is reported unbound even though the macro would
;; bind it.

(define-syntax my-let
  (syntax-rules ()
    [(my-let ([x e:expr]) b:expr)
     (let ([x e]) b)]))

(define y 1)

(my-let ([x]) (+ x y))
